  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\n\nThe central idea is to use a multi-resolution optimization with hierarchical grid sizes and pattern transfer between grids. By varying the grid resolution, we can explore different representations of the step function h(x) that may yield better solutions. This approach is different from the provided code which primarily uses grid upscaling with fixed parent functions. Here, we will systematically test multiple grid sizes independently and transfer successful patterns between different resolutions, potentially avoiding local optima found by the parent-based interpolation method. \n\n2. Mathematical specification\n\nVariables: h(x) as a step function over [0,2] represented by n-point samples.\nConstraints:\n- 0 <= h(i) <= 1 for all i\n- Integral constraint: sum(h) * dx = 1 (where dx=2/n)\n\nObjective: Minimize C_5 = max_k \u222b h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx\n\nRepresentation: h is represented as samples over 3 equally spaced points in [0,2]. Correlation is calculated using 'full' mode.\n\nOptimization method: Multi-resolution gradient descent with grid interpolation and projection.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n\n3.1 Initialization\n- Get initial grid size via parent representation if available\n- Generate random candidate grid if parent grid is not provided or desired\n\n3.2 Resolution exploration\n  3.2.1 Generate candidate grid sizes in increasing order (with base resolution n0, then n1, n2...)\n  3.2.2 For each resolution:\n      - Upscale existing best solution if available (linear interpolation)\n      - Run gradient descent optimization with adaptive learning rate\n      - Store best solution found at each resolution\n\n3.3 Pattern propagation\n  3.3.1 After completing a resolution, interpolate the best solution to the next grid size\n  3.3.2 Use this transferred solution as the initial point for the next optimization\n  3.3.3 Blend random perturbations with the transferred solution\n\n3.4 Optimization function\n  3.4.1 compute_c5_vec(h,n): Compute correlation and return vector of overlap integrals\n  3.4.2 project_feasible(h,n): Maintain h in [0,1] space and enforce integral constraint\n  3.4.3 Objective function: Smooth max of correlation values plus projection penalty\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n\n4.1 Grid choices:\n  - Start with base resolution n_base = 300\n  - Next resolutions: [600, 1800] (quadratic spacing)\n  - Each optimization runs for time proportional to (grid_size / n_base)\n\n4.2 Optimization configuration:\n  - Use hybrid gradient descent with simulated annealing escape barriers\n  - Set learning rate schedule: start high, decay exponentially\n  - Use momentum factor for gradient descent\n  - Always maintain two best solutions: current and best (bayesian restart)\n\n5. Budget and robustness\n\n5.1 Time estimation:\n  - Base optimization: 40% of budget for primary grid (300 samples)\n  - Each grid increase: typically 35% of previous optimization time\n  - Global budget: budget_s (default 1100s) allocated proportionally\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n\n6.1 Validation checks:\n  - Verify 0 <= h[i] <= 1 for all i\n  - Check sum(h)*dx == 1 (integral constraint)\n  - Re-evaluate C_5 using the objective function\n  - Maintain best solution across all resolutions\n\nReturn: (h_values, c5_bound, n_points)",
